{"answer_pre":"Paper 1 (M³VB) computes the geometric median of local posterior distributions. The min-max median aggregation minimizes the maximum KL divergence to any subset posterior, making the central value a geometric median in distribution space. Paper 2 (distributed weighted least squares with median of means) produces a coordinate-wise median. Each coordinate is updated via median-of-means, which selects the median of subset means, yielding an L1-robust central tendency estimator. The mechanism aggregates by taking the median across m block-wise means, minimizing L1 loss in each dimension. Paper 3 (robust meta-analysis via Huber loss and trimming) produces a trimmed mean (M-estimator). The central value comes from iteratively reweighted least squares with Huber loss ρ(r)=(r²/2 for |r|≤k, k|r|-k²/2 otherwise) and study weights wᵢ=ψ(rᵢ)/rᵢ, where ψ is Huber's score function. Outlier studies are downweighted or trimmed, yielding an M-estimator that is asymptotically normal but more robust than the mean. The aggregation mechanism (Huber loss and iterative reweighting) determines that the central value is a smooth compromise between mean (quadratic loss) and median (absolute loss).","evidence_pages":[70,71,72,73,74,75,76,77,78,79,80,81,82,83,84,85]}
