{"answer_pre":"RLGP achieves robustness through quantile-thresholding and iterative outlier trimming, which removes atypical observations from the local neighborhood based on a sparsity parameter q before fitting the GP. The ALDRM (Adaptive Local Density-based Robust Model, referred to as laGP in the papers) achieves robustness through adaptive subset selection with mean squared predictive error criterion, iteratively expanding the neighborhood while filtering out poor contributors to prediction accuracy. JGP (Jump Gaussian Process) achieves robustness through explicit partitioning using hyperplane or quadratic functions to segment the input space into regions with homogeneous behavior, fitting separate GPs to each partition. Despite these structurally distinct mechanisms—trimming/filtering, iterative adaptive selection, and explicit segmentation—all three share the common statistical principle of bounded influence or limited sensitivity to individual observations: they prevent any single atypical point or small subset of anomalous data from dominating the local model fit by either excluding them, down-weighting their contribution, or isolating them in separate partitions.","evidence_pages":[58,59,60,61,62,63,64,65,66,67,68]}
```

