{"answer_pre":"In the Gibbs/HMC seismic SBD sampler (Paper 1), the specific mechanism causing failure to represent all modes is that the Markov chain can become trapped in local modes and fail to explore the full multimodal posterior when energy barriers between modes are high relative to the finite step size and limited burn-in time. Paper 2 (discretization approximation) handles multiplicity by allowing competing MAP histories to coexist at finite Θ: as Θ→0 the Gibbs measure concentrates on minimum-action histories, selecting a set of MAP histories that reflect metastable admissible paths rather than exploring a single mode. The framework explicitly accounts for multiple competing minimum-action solutions through the partition function summing over all admissible histories. Paper 3 (Bayesian-inference-over-histories) addresses multiplicity through the Gibbs-type probability measure with temperature-like parameter Θ that controls epistemic uncertainty: at finite Θ the measure spreads probability over multiple admissible histories weighted by exp(-A/Θ), naturally representing competing solutions; as Θ→0 it concentrates on the minimum-action history (or histories) emerging as the MAP solution(s), handling coexistence of metastable paths through the probabilistic framework rather than requiring iterative exploration.","evidence_pages":[14,15,16,17,18,19,71,72,73,74,75,76,77,78,79]}
