{"answer_pre":"The RPF neural solver evaluates in approximately 30 microseconds per power flow problem once trained (page 9). The GAN-LSTM anomaly detector processes 60-hour windows at test time using latent-space optimization with frozen networks; while inference through the LSTM is fast, the latent optimization for each window adds computational overhead, though the paper does not report explicit per-window timing (pages 13-14). The WPT+HOS Mahalanobis detector computes a signature via wavelet packet transform residual extraction, third-order cumulant estimation, and a quadratic Mahalanobis distance evaluation; the paper does not provide per-sample timing, but the method is designed for frame-level real-time processing with low latency (pages 17-19, 23). Comparing computational demands: the RPF solver is extremely fast at inference (30 μs), making it best suited for low-latency streaming decisions. The GAN-LSTM requires per-window latent optimization, which is slower than direct forward-pass inference. The WPT+HOS method involves wavelet decomposition and higher-order statistics computation per frame, which is lightweight but more complex than a simple neural forward pass. For real-time streaming with minimal latency, the RPF neural solver is best suited due to its sub-millisecond evaluation time. The WPT+HOS detector is suitable for near-real-time monitoring at the frame level (hourly windows). The GAN-LSTM, requiring iterative optimization per test window, is less suited to strict low-latency streaming than the other two.","evidence_pages":[9,13,14,17,18,19,23]}
