{"answer_pre":"In nodewise graph estimation (Paper 1), the ambiguity is resolved by separating transient anomalies from persistent structure through a change-point detection framework that identifies structural breaks (jumps) in the conditional mean sequence over time. The method uses hypothesis tests (CUSUM, sup-norm LRT, BinSeg) to distinguish genuine regime shifts from noise, allowing stable estimation within each detected regime while explicitly modeling jumps between them. In stream regression (Paper 2), the distinction is operationalized via observation-level indicator variables that adaptively activate or deactivate data points based on their consistency with the underlying regression structure. The method uses likelihood-ratio tests with mixture models to decide whether each observation belongs to the nominal regression (persistent structure) or represents an anomaly, with posterior probabilities determining inclusion. This avoids conflating outliers with true signal by down-weighting inconsistent data. In local surrogate modeling (Paper 3/RLGP), the ambiguity is addressed through quantile-thresholding combined with iterative outlier identification in the nearest-neighbor set. The algorithm identifies observations inconsistent with the local Gaussian process structure (transient anomalies) by flagging those whose residuals exceed adaptive quantile bounds, then excludes them to refine the neighborhood. A robust mean-shift t-distributed likelihood further down-weights anomalies, ensuring the fitted surrogate captures only the persistent local structure while being robust to rogue points from neighborhood selection errors or sensor drift.","evidence_pages":[6,7,8,9,26,28,29,30,46,47,48,49,50,51,58]}
