{"answer_pre":"In logistic regression using MLE for binary survey outcomes, robustness to outliers requires separate diagnostic procedures (z-square residuals, Pearson residuals, deviance residuals, scatter diagrams) because the MLE objective maximizes the likelihood function without built-in outlier resistance. In contrast, quantile regression uses check-loss (asymmetric absolute value loss) as its estimation objective, which is inherently robust to outliers due to its L1-norm property, building robustness directly into the model framework rather than requiring post-hoc diagnostics. OLS mean regression using squared-error loss (L2-norm) is similarly sensitive to outliers like logistic MLE and requires separate diagnostic checks. The check-loss framework provides robust estimation across the conditional distribution, while logistic MLE for binary outcomes focuses on maximizing probability of observed data, making it sensitive to extreme observations that can disproportionately affect the likelihood. This reveals that the choice between L1-based (check-loss, quantile) versus likelihood-based (MLE, logistic) objectives fundamentally determines whether outlier resistance is an inherent property of the estimation framework or must be addressed through additional validation steps.","evidence_pages":[3,4,7,55,56,57,58,59,60,61,62,63,64,65,66,67,68]}
```

