{"answer_pre":"The experimental results reveal that both zeroth-order split federated learning and quantum-classical federated learning demonstrate robustness to variations in auxiliary network complexity. In Paper 1, zeroth-order split federated learning shows that the auxiliary network (the server-side network in split learning architecture) complexity affects communication overhead but not the core learning capability, as convergence rates depend primarily on the local client-side gradient estimation quality and the dimension of the main model parameters rather than the auxiliary network size. The bounds in Theorem 1 show convergence rates of O(√(d/(hNBT))) + O(1/√(dhNBT)), where d is the parameter dimension of the main model, independent of auxiliary network complexity. In Paper 3, quantum-classical federated learning (QFed) demonstrates that auxiliary network complexity can be dramatically reduced through quantum compression during training while maintaining competitive accuracy. The experiments show a 77.6% reduction in parameter count (from 6,690 to 1,497 parameters) when using quantum-train compression compared to classical VGG-like models, while achieving 84% accuracy versus 86% for the classical baseline on FashionMNIST. The quantum neural network serves as a parameter-efficient auxiliary structure during training, with the final classical model used for inference requiring no quantum hardware. This informs resource-efficient federated architecture design by indicating that: (1) auxiliary networks can be kept lightweight without sacrificing convergence properties, reducing communication costs; (2) quantum compression during training enables polylogarithmic parameter reduction while preserving learning capacity; and (3) the hybrid quantum-classical paradigm allows substantial model compression during federated training phases while maintaining fully classical inference, making quantum advantages accessible without quantum deployment infrastructure.","eviden