{"answer_pre":"Paper 1 uses the algebraic property that the Lanczos-generated Krylov basis {|Pₙ⟩} becomes a good basis for the Hamiltonian when qₙₙ = qₙz = qzz, meaning the Hamiltonian crossings satisfy specific equalities. This criterion identifies integrable configurations by demanding that the Krylov basis structure aligns with the Hamiltonian's tridiagonal form. The limitation reported is that deviations from qₙₙ = qₙz = qzz (i.e., |qₙₙ - qₙz| ~ dz ≪ 1) require corrections to the Krylov basis, and the error grows as quantified by the relative error ⟨ℰₙ₊₁|ℰₙ₊₁⟩/⟨Pₙ₊₁|Pₙ₊₁⟩, indicating the degree to which the same Krylov basis approximately describes systems away from the integrable case. Paper 2 uses the analytic property that the Lax connection ℒ(x) has specific pole/zero divisor structure that is preserved under reflection. Integrability is determined by requiring the divisors of zeros Dz[ℒ(x)] and poles Dp[ℒ(x)] to be permuted by the reflection ρ(x) that accompanies the spatial reflection, while also demanding that ℒ(x) and the gauge-transformed ℒᵖᵇ(ρ(x)) have local expansions at marked points x₀, x'₀. This fixes ρ(x) and U from the divisor structure. The limitation is that this criterion alone does not fix ρ(x) at nontrivial NSNS flux without additional constraints; moreover, when poles and zeros cannot be permuted consistently (as shown in Table I cases 1-3), no solution exists, restricting which configurations belong to the integrable class.","evidence_pages":[32,39,40,41,42,43]}
