{"answer_pre":"Whether critical current suppression is quantified by binding energy renormalization (EB/EJ) or escape temperature (Tesc = ℏωp/7.2kB) is determined by the Stewart-McCumber parameter βc. For underdamped junctions (βc > 1), corresponding to ferromagnetic junctions with large capacitance and high plasma frequency, phase escapes occur from a well-defined metastable state at current Isw controlled by thermal or quantum activation over a barrier ΔU ≈ EJ, with the barrier height set by EB/EJ renormalization. For overdamped junctions (βc < 1), typical of STM junctions with very low capacitance, there is no barrier and phase diffuses continuously at current Is, with suppression governed by the escape rate at effective temperature Tesc. The ferromagnetic junctions are underdamped with βc ≫ 1, placing them in the activation-dominated regime quantified by EB/EJ, while STM junctions are overdamped with βc ≪ 1, placing them in the diffusive regime quantified by Tesc.","evidence_pages":[19,20,21,22,23,24,25,26]}
```

