{"answer_pre":"Manhattan-style multilayer junctions have large electrode overlap areas resulting in junction capacitances of ~1 fF, yielding EC ~ 100 μeV and requiring EJ/EC ≳ 10² to reach the quantum regime with negligible charge fluctuations. In contrast, STM tip-sample junctions have extremely small effective areas (~1 nm²) producing capacitances of ~1 aF, three orders of magnitude smaller, resulting in EC ~ 100 meV. This allows quantum operation at moderate EJ/EC ~ 0.1-10, accessible with realistic junction transparencies. The STM geometry's lower capacitance shifts the parameter space to higher EC, enabling quantum coherent transport and multiple Andreev reflection signatures at experimentally achievable junction resistances (1-100 kΩ), whereas Manhattan junctions require much lower resistances and higher transparencies to achieve comparable EJ/EC ratios.","evidence_pages":[19,20,21,22,23,24,25,26,27]}
