{"answer_pre":"Paper 1's R² merging technique is a distinct mechanism from Paper 2's Lemma 2.3 local-boundary-connectedness condition. Paper 1 relies on closure-finiteness to ensure that gluing three neighboring section/sector functions across a shared boundary point produces a well-defined continuous function—the closures are unions of finitely many cells, so boundary points lie in only finitely many cell closures. Paper 2's Lemma 2.3 requires local boundary connectedness of the merged sector A∪B∪C to ensure that the closure of the middle section B equals the intersection of the closures of A and C. Paper 1 does not invoke or require local boundary connectedness; its 2-dimensional argument works because closure-finiteness suffices in R² for the needed topological control. This distinction is critical: Paper 1's technique cannot be directly reused to justify the R⁴ counterexamples of Paper 2, because those R⁴ examples are closure-finite but not well-bordered, meaning cell boundaries can have complex arrangements not controlled by closure-finiteness alone. The R⁴ counterexamples deliberately exploit the failure of local boundary connectedness and well-borderedness beyond dimension 2, conditions that Paper 1's simpler 2D setting did not need to address.","evidence_pages":[1,2,3,4,5,6,27,33,34,35,36,37,38]}
```

CQA_04a617c5d063ff3e.answer_pre_raw:
```json
{"answer_pre":"Paper 1 (MMOT) uses the Sinkhorn algorithm (iterative martingale projection) to secure existence of the martingale measure consistent with prescribed marginals. This is a dynamical device: it iteratively enforces the martingale constraint E[X_{t+1}|X_t] = X_t via Lagrange multipliers and dual potential updates until convergence. Paper 2 (neural approximation of MMOT) uses a physics-informed training loss with a martingale constraint term that penalizes drift violations computed via the Gibbs kernel induced by the neural potentials. This is a distributional device: the loss enforces the global martingale condition by measuring empirical drift E_P[X_{t+1}|X_t] - x_m at each grid point and penalizing deviations, ensuring the learned measure satisfies the martingale property. The two devices differ fundamentally: Sinkhorn is an iterative projection algorithm operating on discrete measures (dynamical/algorithmic), while the martingale constraint loss is a soft penalty integrated into neural network training that enforces distributional consistency (distributional/learning-based).","evidence_pages":[3,4,5,9,10,11,12]}
```

