CQA_1edba5eaffba5189.answer_pre:
Paper 2's WWF-based method reconstructs an effective Bloch projector Π_k from spectroscopic intensities, and from this it derives an effective quantum geometric tensor whose real part gives the quantum metric g_ij (and hence its momentum derivatives, i.e. the quantum metric quadrupole ∂∂g_ij that enters σ^LPL/σ^CPL in Paper 1). Since this g_ij is obtained purely from projector overlaps, Paper 2's spectroscopic method can in principle recover this quadrupole contribution.

However, Paper 1's weighted quantum metric G_aj = 4ħ Re[Σ_{m≠n} (A_nm)_a(A_mn)_j/(ε_m−ε_n)] contains an explicit interband energy-denominator weighting that is not part of a purely momentum-space projector-based QGT. Paper 2's effective QGT is constructed only from the projector Π_k (band identity/overlap information), not from the energy differences between all bands m,n weighted in this specific way; it yields the "quasi-metric" analogous to g_ij but does not supply the 1/(ε_m−ε_n) weighted combination defining G_aj. Thus Paper 2's spectroscopic scheme can recover the quantum metric quadrupole term but cannot, in principle, directly recover the weighted quantum metric G_aj without additional energy-resolved input beyond the projector construction — so it cannot fully reconstruct both quantities entering the inverse Faraday/Cotton-Mouton responses.

{"answer_pre":"Paper 2's effective QGT is built solely from the momentum-space band projector Π_k, so it directly yields an effective quantum metric g_ij (and its momentum derivatives), which is exactly the quantum metric quadrupole term (∫[dq] εij ∂∂g_jb) needed in Paper 1's σ^LPL/σ^CPL formulas. However, Paper 1's second required quantity, the weighted quantum metric G_aj = 4ħRe[Σ_{m≠n}(A_nm)_a(A_mn)_j/(ε_m−ε_n)], contains an explicit interband energy-denominator weighting that arises from perturbative corrections to the Bloch states, not from projector overlaps alone. Since Paper 2's construction only uses Π_k (topological/geometric overlap information) and does not encode this specific energy-weighted interband sum, it cannot, in principle, reconstruct G_aj the same way it reconstructs g_ij. So Paper 2's spectroscopic method can recover the quantum metric quadrupole contribution to the inverse Faraday/Cotton–Mouton response, but not the weighted quantum metric G_aj contribution, meaning it cannot fully recover both quantities.","evidence_pages":[2,3,4,20]}

