{"answer_pre":"RLGP's robustification framework is compatible with S3N's covariance structure. RLGP's mean-shift robustification uses an outlier indicator vector γ to adaptively shift the local mean, leaving the covariance structure unchanged. S3N defines covariance through stream distance and tail-up/tail-down models, which RLGP can accept as the base covariance function c(x,x';θ). The determining feature is that RLGP operates on the mean component via γ without requiring modification to the covariance kernel, while S3N provides a valid covariance function based on stream topology. RLGP's iterative quantile-thresholding identifies outliers within any nearest-neighbor set, including stream-distance neighborhoods that mix ecologically distinct branches, by down-weighting observations with large residuals regardless of their spatial or topological provenance.","evidence_pages":[60,61,62,63,64,65,66,67,68,69,70,71,72,73,74,75]}
