{"answer_pre":"Yes, both studies independently converge on mitochondrial-related genes and processes as functionally important. Paper 1 (GNN cancer prediction study) identifies through over-representation analysis that supernode 25 is significantly enriched for mitochondrial respiratory chain complex assembly (GO:0033108, enrichment ratio 49.153, FDR <2.2e-16), NADH dehydrogenase complex assembly (GO:0010257, enrichment ratio 73.730, FDR <2.2e-16), and other mitochondrial processes including nucleoside triphosphate metabolic process, proton transmembrane transport, and ATP hydrolysis coupled cation transmembrane transport. Paper 2 (gene regulatory network knock-in study) identifies Ppara, Phox2b, Esrra, Med23, and Ppargc1b as highly restorative genes, with three of the top five genes (Ppara, Esrra, and Ppargc1b) directly connected to mitochondrial function. The convergence of these entirely different approaches—one using graph neural networks on protein-protein interaction networks for cancer classification, the other using information theory on gene regulatory networks for aging—on mitochondrial processes suggests that mitochondrial regulation represents a fundamental network-level signal that is detectable across different biological contexts, network architectures, and analytical frameworks. This indicates mitochondrial function serves as a critical hub in cellular information processing whose dysregulation manifests as measurable changes in network-level properties regardless of the specific modeling approach used.","evidence_pages":[10,19,20,21]}
